"Benjamin Lewis" <bclewis@cs.sfu.ca> wrote:

> Kent Paul Dolan wrote:

>> while ( ( fooListWalker != ( (FooStructLinkType) NULL ) ) == true )

> Why on earth would anyone include the last test
> ( == true ), when != is already a boolean operator?

Try to envision the additional help the last
equality test gives to someone whose prior
experience used as "not equals" operator:

   <>
   /=
   =/=
   .ne.

and I'm sure many more I don't know personally. The
idea is deliberate overkill to make sure the _human
reader_ groks the code, the compiler was more than
happy with "while(curr)", as everyone has agreed.

Saying that the construct has already reached the
stage the compiler needs isn't implying that it has
yet reached a form sufficient to educate the middle
four sigmas of the coding population.

Somehow, humans have rather major problems with
"software in the large", and so Arianne(sp?) plows
into the dirt (bad test plan choices), a Mars probe
trys to go through the planet (mixed units English
and Metric crossing a software interface boundary
where neither coder side checked what units the
other side was using), some X-Ray machines
mistakenly burn holes through the patients in lethal
localities (cause unremembered), and so on and so
forth.

Obsessively tutorial code is one small facet of the
effort to address this reality of human weakness in
handling overwhelming complexity at any stage in the
software process from requirements gathering to
executing the software.

My code snippet isn't nearly as far as you can go in
that direction. Respected (worshipped by quite a few
of us) computer scientist Donald Knuth, author of
the textbook series "The Art of Computer
Programming", inventor of TeX and METAFONT and
grandfather of LaTeX, and far more accomplishements
than I know, much less can list, recommends a whole
other level of the same medicine, which he calls
"Literate Programming", in which the entire text
genesis of the software is an intimate part of the
source code.

One place for the interested reader to follow this
whole area of human enterprise, last time I looked,
is to subscribe to newsgroup comp.risks. You'll fall
off your chair laughing, a _lot_, at human caused
software stupidities, but with luck you'll reflect
at least a little on how on earth our existing
practices let those stupidities slip through to
production code, and perhaps thereby achieve
the level of enlightenment that says the prima donna
versions of those practices aren't sacred and need
replacement.

